WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our next guest is a comedian
and actress who created,

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.303
wrote, and stars
in the critically

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:05.505
acclaimed series "Starstruck."

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:08.007
The first two seasons are
streaming now on HBO Max.

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:09.175
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.610
-Why did you send me a letter?
-No, no, no.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.315
Okay, so, I may have sent
a few letters to people

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:20.086
when I thought I was leaving
the country forever.

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:23.523
-And what sort of emotional
space were you in at the time?

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:27.227
-[ Sobbing ]

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:34.434
Not a great one.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.503
-Please welcome to the show
Rose Matafeo!

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:40.339
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:48.247 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:52.452 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:54.020
-Hello!
-Welcome to the show, Rose.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.788
-Thanks for having me.
-This is a fantastic show

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.657
with just a wonderful premise.

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.025
-Oh, yes, wonderful.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.392
-You have a one-night stand
with someone,

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:02.128
not realizing
that they're a movie star.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.696
-Yes, she's not
wearing her glasses.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:05.197
-Yeah.
-That's what I say.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:06.298
-That's a beautiful twist.

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:07.766
-Beautiful twist
that I put her in.

00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:09.802
-But that sounds like such
a wonderful idea.

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:11.404
Was it an idea
that you always had?

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.805
Was it the first idea you had?

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:14.006
-It was not.

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:16.342
Well, well, well, I pitched
a bunch of ideas

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:19.545
for shows that I could
potentially be in myself,

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:23.149
and they were all
deeply rejected

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:25.384
by every channel in the U.K.

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:27.720
And then, on the way back
to New Zealand from London,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.422
I was on a long flight.

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.024
It's a very long flights,
it's like 24 hours.

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:32.492
And my agent was like,
"Just please,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.493
just pitch one more thing."

00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:36.429
And I was quite drunk,

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:40.466
And I was like, "Well, maybe
I'm in a rom-com, then."

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:41.567
[ Laughs ]

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:43.635
And then I wrote
like a page of stuff,

00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:45.371
and, yeah,
that's where it started.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.173
I made all the people on
the plane act it out.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.308
I was very disruptive.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:51.277
IT was like,
"Get her off the flight."

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.644
But, yeah.
-It paid off.

00:01:52.644 --> 00:01:55.514
And, you know, so, it's about
this relationship between

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:57.183
these two characters, and your
boyfriend in the show

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.618
gets stopped a lot
because he's famous.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:01.520
And now two seasons
of a very wonderful show,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.588
are you getting stopped at all?

00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:03.957
-It's very generous
of you to ask.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:05.491
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:07.927
I -- it did happen,
because it happened recently,

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.229
like, about two weeks ago.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.831
Was Saint Patrick's Day.
I was here in New York.

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:14.400
-Yeah, right place to be.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.168
-Whoa!
-Yeah.

00:02:16.168 --> 00:02:18.538
-You guys go hard.
-Yeah.

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.106
We had a couple of years
with no parades.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.207
-Really?
-Yeah, so we had

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.642
to bring them back.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.010
-I had this wonderful thing
where the parade was going on,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:27.213
and then a perpendicular parade
was happening of angry

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:28.948
New Yorkers trying
to cross the street.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:31.985
Yeah.
-It was -- it the real New York.

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.320
It was great,
but I walked out of a cafe,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.756
and this girl, God bless her,
the drunkest girl

00:02:37.756 --> 00:02:38.958
I've ever seen in my life,

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:40.292
she was walking down
the street.

00:02:40.292 --> 00:02:42.629
She turned around, and she was
like, "'Starstruck'!"

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:44.263
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:46.199
"'Starstruck,' when is the next
series out?"

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:47.800
And I was like, "Next week."

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.102
She was like,
"Good, see you there."

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:50.670
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.606
And I was, like, I cannot tell
you how little

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:56.709
her eyes were open
to even recognize me.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.611
And it was so sweet that I got
recognized by --

00:02:58.611 --> 00:02:59.812
and it was really weird.

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:01.314
-That's the level of fame
you're at now.

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:02.515
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:05.084
The eye doesn't even have
to be fully open.

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.519
-She sensed me.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.587
She sensed I was around,
but, you know...

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:11.691
-So, obviously, you're from
New Zealand, live in London.

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:14.226
When was the last time
you were in New York City?

00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:16.796
-The last time?
I'm an adult now.

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:18.864
-Yeah.
-But I wasn't before.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.433
That was a weird way
to get into a story.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:23.035
I was 16 when I was here last.

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:24.637
-Okay.
-I was on a school history trip.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:25.638
-Oh, wow.

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.440
-I do not know how
we related it

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.842
to history in any way,
but it was amazing.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:33.746
I was 16, and it was like
mid-2000, and the teachers

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:36.616
let us as, 16-year-old girls,
all girls school,

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:39.952
just roam about
New York city alone...

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:41.654
-Yeah.
...for an entire day.

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:44.090
I think, because Ms. Carter,
who was running the trip,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.625
she was really into
"Project Runway"

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:48.861
at that time, and she really
wanted to go to Mood Fabrics.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.396
-Okay.
-And so she was like,

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.064
"Girls, enjoy the city."

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:56.301
And I got lost in Macy's.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.301 --> 00:04:00.173
My friends put a missing-person
report at Macy's for me,

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:01.641
which is apparently a thing.

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:04.644
And they drew, like, a cartoon
of me to try and describe me.

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:05.978
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:07.547
-How long were you lost?

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:09.481
-Seven hours.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.481 --> 00:04:11.784
No, not long at all.
It was really weird.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.085
-Long enough for a drawing?

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:14.920
-It was really weird.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.356
But, yeah, that was fun.

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:19.525
So now, yeah, I've had a much
more adult time in New York.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:20.793
-Yeah, that's good.

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:23.095
I hope nobody's letting
you go to Macy's alone.

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:24.997
-I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:27.399
People recognize me at Macy's.
Alright?

00:04:27.399 --> 00:04:29.768
They know who I am.
-So, there are obviously

00:04:29.768 --> 00:04:32.505
some really lovely New Zealand
elements in the show.

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.006
Scrumpy hands
was not a thing.

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:36.642
When I heard this, I did not
know what Scrumpy hands was.

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:37.709
I'm gonna assume
a lot of people here don't.

00:04:37.709 --> 00:04:38.911
Explain Scrumpy hands.

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:41.113
-No one knows what Scrumpy hands
is, beyond like

00:04:41.113 --> 00:04:42.381
10 people in New Zealand.

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:43.916
No, it is a New Zealand thing.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.952
It's a New Zealand tradition,
a great tradition,

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:49.588
where you take two very cheap
bottles of cider

00:04:49.588 --> 00:04:53.526
and duct-tape them
to a hand each,

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:56.395
and you have to drink them
until they're finished.

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:58.431
And that's our culture.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:04:59.665
-Yeah.

00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:01.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:02.635
-It's sort of like a party.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:04.270
It's like a sort
of a party thing.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.305
-Yeah, well, I would hope
it's not a work thing.

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:07.840
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.308
I know, it's such
a church thing, as well.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:10.409
Yeah, I learnt that at church.

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:12.144
-What does "Scrumpy" refer to,
though?

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:13.579
-Scrumpy is a brand of cider.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:14.647
-Okay, gotcha.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.149
-I'm sponsored by Scrumpy,
obviously.

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:17.883
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:19.418
-We do need you to say
"hashtag ad" after everything.

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:21.354
-Hashtag add.
Hashtag on Scrumpy.

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:22.721
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.721 --> 00:05:24.556
Scrumpy hands, great.

00:05:24.556 --> 00:05:26.926
-There's a really wonderful,
deeply funny

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:29.194
pregnancy-test scene
in the show.

00:05:29.194 --> 00:05:32.465
-Yes, yes, and serious,
too, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:34.733
-When you write
something like that,

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:38.671
when you get that idea, are you
like, "Oh, this is a winner"?

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.006
-Yeah, well, there the --
the worst thing about that

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:42.675
was, a lot of people were
commenting on it being like,

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:43.943
"This is hilarious.

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:47.479
What an idiot this character is.

00:05:47.479 --> 00:05:48.814
Oh, my goodness."

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:51.850
And especially someone reading
a premise which is wrong.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.353
And I was like,
"That's straight.

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:56.722
That happened.
Uhh, complete.

00:05:56.722 --> 00:05:59.726
Like, there was no acting.
It was a re-creation.

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:03.429
It was a re-creation of me
reading a pregnancy test wrong,

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:06.799
bursting into tears,
realizing I just read

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:11.337
the wrong lines, and moving on
as if nothing happened, like,

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:13.206
just recovering so quickly.

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:14.640
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.843
And that's the weird thing,
is that people watch the show

00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:18.711
and they're like,
"Jessie is an idiot."

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:21.747
And I'm like, "Yeah, she is."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:25.351
"What's she doing
all that stuff for?"

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.653
So it's a bit close
to the bone sometimes.

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:30.422
-You've done stand-up comedy,
but you were on television

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:32.558
at a very young age,
sort of before your stand-up.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:33.492
So, what age is it?

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:35.727
What's "Sticky T.V."

00:06:35.727 --> 00:06:37.497
-Who are your researchers?

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:41.300
Because that's the deepest cut
of all time.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.901
I started stand-up
when I was 15,

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:45.037
but when I was 12,
I worked at

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.738
a children's television show,

00:06:46.738 --> 00:06:48.908
as I gave advice
to other children.

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:49.909
-Okay.

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:51.210
[ Laughter ]

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.712
-It was so weird.
-Yeah.

00:06:52.712 --> 00:06:55.781
-A 12-year-old taking advice
from an 11-year-old.

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:57.450
-Yeah.
-Like, it was bizarre.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.085
And I used to open fan mail

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:01.787
at the production company
after school.

00:07:01.787 --> 00:07:03.155
Really weird.

00:07:03.155 --> 00:07:06.592
One kid sent in fingernail
clippings and dried chilies.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.360
-That was what he sent
in his fan mail?

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.728
-For one of the female
presenters.

00:07:09.728 --> 00:07:10.863
-Okay, gotcha.

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:12.665
And then he went on a list,
I'm hoping?

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:14.867
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah,
yeah, yeah.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:16.935
No, I'm going out with him now.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:16.935 --> 00:07:18.837
I keep track of him.

00:07:18.837 --> 00:07:21.574
No, yeah, it was really weird,
it was really weird.

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:25.277
-And you also, you know,
obviously, this would count,

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:26.546
the technical term
for what you're doing

00:07:26.546 --> 00:07:27.946
about your show is a junket.

00:07:27.946 --> 00:07:29.348
You're going out, you're getting
interviewed about your show.

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:32.318
But you were on this side
with American actors

00:07:32.318 --> 00:07:34.253
not too long ago.
-Not too long ago.

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:36.789
When I was like early 20s,
I interviewed people.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:39.725
I interview -- for New Zealand
television, I did, like,

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:42.761
Amy Poehler and Amy Schumer
and Bill Hader,

00:07:42.761 --> 00:07:45.631
and it's very --
it's very weird.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.667
The funniest thing, though,
is that I would do bits.

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:49.968
Like, I'd do bits
like I'd speed-date them.

00:07:49.968 --> 00:07:51.604
So I'd bring, like,
a mini little table,

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:54.340
a restaurant table,
and, like, be dressed up.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:55.340
And then I'd, at one point,
be like,

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.843
"Do you have any questions
for me?"

00:07:57.843 --> 00:08:00.179
To, like, literal celebrities.

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:01.280
The best thing
is that when I did

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.915
Bill Hader, it was really fun.

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:05.318
At the end, he was like,
"Yeah, when they told me

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:07.720
about this, I thought
it'd be really bad,

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.889
but I was actually
kind of okay."

00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:11.057
[ Laughter ]

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:12.992
I was 22, and I was like,
"Thank you, Bill Hader."

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:14.193
[ Laughter ]

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:15.595
I was so nervous.

00:08:15.595 --> 00:08:18.063
Yes, it's worse
being on this side.

00:08:18.063 --> 00:08:20.132
-I agree with you.
-How are you doing?

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:22.167
Does anyone ask you questions?

00:08:22.167 --> 00:08:25.371
-No.
-Are you doing okay?

00:08:25.371 --> 00:08:26.572
-I'm okay now.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:27.873
-Okay, we need to go.
We need to wrap it up.

00:08:27.873 --> 00:08:29.141
-We're gonna wrap it up.
-We need to wrap that up.

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:31.276
-Hey, thank you so much.
Congratulations on the show.

00:08:31.276 --> 00:08:33.011
It's really lovely meeting you
and having you here.

00:08:33.011 --> 00:08:35.448
Guys, that's Rose Matafeo.

00:08:35.448 --> 00:08:37.016
"Starstruck" is streaming now
on HBO Max.

00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:38.251
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

